1. Which Will Print?
For the following makeSound() and move() methods being called in the main method, what statements will print?

class Animal {
    void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Some generic animal sound");
    }

    static void move() {
        System.out.println("Animals move in various ways");
    }
}

class Bird extends Animal {
    @Override
    void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Bird is chirping");
    }

    static void move() {
        System.out.println("Birds fly");
    }
}

class Penguin extends Bird {
    @Override
    void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Penguin is honking");
    }

    static void move() {
        System.out.println("Penguins waddle");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Animal animal1 = new Penguin();
        Bird bird1 = new Penguin();
        Animal animal2 = new Bird();

        animal1.makeSound();  // 1
        bird1.makeSound();    // 2
        animal2.makeSound();  // 3

        animal1.move();       // 4
        bird1.move();         // 5
        animal2.move();       // 6
    }
}
2. Overriding the toString() Method
Objective: Design a class representing a Movie and override the toString() method to provide a meaningful string representation of a movie object.

Instructions:
Create a class Movie with the following attributes:
String title
String director
int releaseYear
double rating (on a scale of 1 to 10)
Override the toString() method to return a formatted string that includes the movie’s title, director, release year, and rating in a readable format.
In the main method, create two Movie objects and print them to verify that the toString() method works correctly.
3. Using Enums for Traffic Light States
 Create an Enum to represent the states of a traffic light and implement a method to determine the time duration for each state.

Instructions:
Create an enum called TrafficLight with the following constants:
RED
YELLOW
GREEN
Implement a method in the TrafficLight enum called getDuration() that returns the duration (in seconds) for each light:
RED should return 30 seconds
YELLOW should return 5 seconds
GREEN should return 25 seconds
