4. Inner Classes for a Library System
Objective: Create a simple library system that uses different types of inner classes to demonstrate their purposes.

Instructions:
Outer Class: Create an outer class called Library that contains the following:
A private member variable name (String) representing the library's name.
A method printLibraryName() that prints the library name.
Static Nested Class: Inside the Library class, create a static nested class called Book with:
Private member variables title (String) and author (String).
A constructor that initializes the title and author.
A method getBookDetails() that returns a string containing the book's title and author.
Inner Class: Inside the Library class, create a non-static inner class called Member with:
Private member variables memberId (int) and memberName (String).
A constructor that initializes the memberId and memberName.
A method getMemberDetails() that returns a string containing the member's ID and name.
Local Inner Class: Inside the Library class, implement a method called createBookInfo() that creates a local inner class called BookInfo. This class should:
Have a private member variable book of type Book.
A constructor that takes a Book object as a parameter and assigns it to the book variable.
A method displayBookInfo() that prints the details of the book.
Demonstration: In the main method of your program:
Create a Library object and print the library name.
Create a Book object and print its details.
Create a Member object and print its details.
Call the createBookInfo() method to display the information of the book.
5. Design Pattern PracticeExamples
1: Singleton Pattern
Implement the Singleton design pattern to ensure only one instance of a class exists.

Create a Logger class that logs messages to the console. Ensure that this class can only be instantiated once. Provide a static method getInstance() to access the single instance. Demonstrate that any attempt to create another instance returns the same object.

Instructions:

Create a class named Logger.
Implement a private static instance of the class.
Create a private constructor to prevent instantiation from outside.
Implement the getInstance() method that returns the single instance.
Add a method log(String message) that prints the message to the console.
Demonstrate that the same instance is returned when getInstance() is called multiple times.
2: Builder Pattern
Use the Builder design pattern to construct complex objects step by step.

Design a Pizza class that includes properties like size, crustType, and toppings. Implement a PizzaBuilder class to build a Pizza object using method chaining. Allow the user to customize the pizza by selecting various options before building the final object. Provide a build() method to create the Pizza instance.

Instructions:

Create a class named Pizza with fields size, crustType, and toppings.
Implement a PizzaBuilder class with methods to set each property and a build() method that creates a Pizza instance.
Use method chaining to allow the builder to be used fluently.
3: Factory Pattern
Use the Factory design pattern to create objects without specifying the exact class of the object that will be created.

Create a SnackFactory class that produces different types of snacks, such as Chips, Candy, and Chocolate. Each snack should have a method getDescription() that returns a string describing the snack. Use the factory to create snacks based on user input (like snack type) without exposing the instantiation logic.

Instructions:

Create an interface named Snack with a method getDescription().
Implement classes Chips, Candy, and Chocolate that implement the Snack interface.
Create a SnackFactory class with a static method createSnack(String type) to return the appropriate snack object based on the input type.
Demonstrate the factory pattern by creating snacks using the factory.
4: Immutable Object
Design an immutable object to ensure that its state cannot be modified after creation.

Create a Color class representing a color with properties red, green, and blue. Make the class immutable by providing a constructor to initialize the properties and only getter methods to access the values. Implement a method adjustBrightness(double factor) that returns a new Color object with adjusted brightness without changing the original object.

Instructions:

Create a class named Color with fields red, green, and blue.
Implement a constructor to initialize the properties.
Provide getter methods for red, green, and blue.
Implement a method adjustBrightness(double factor) that returns a new Color with adjusted brightness without modifying the original object.